ADS-005 — Decision Record (DR)

Document ID: ADS-005
Title: Decision Record (DR)
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Decision Record (DR), the canonical representation of an engineering decision within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

Every engineering decision, recommendation approval, rejection, or revision SHALL be represented as a Decision Record.

The Decision Record serves as the authoritative and auditable record of engineering decision-making.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Decision model
Decision hierarchy
Decision lifecycle
Decision metadata
Decision governance
Decision traceability
Decision quality
Decision approval

This specification applies to every engineering decision produced within Atlas.

3. Canonical Definition

A Decision Record (DR) is a specialization of the Canonical Atlas Object that captures an engineering decision, its justification, supporting evidence, approvals, alternatives, and lifecycle in a governed and traceable form.

4. Objectives

The Decision Record SHALL:

Record engineering decisions.
Preserve engineering rationale.
Preserve evidence.
Preserve approvals.
Preserve traceability.
Support auditing.
Support revision history.
5. Inheritance

Decision Record inherits every mandatory property from the Canonical Atlas Object.

Canonical Atlas Object
        │
        ▼
 Decision Record
        │
 ┌──────┼────────────┐
 ▼      ▼            ▼
Recommendation  Approval  Revision
6. Decision Types

Atlas defines the following canonical decision types.

Decision Type	Description
Recommendation	Proposed engineering action
Approval	Accepted recommendation
Rejection	Declined recommendation
Override	Human decision replacing system recommendation
Revision	Updated engineering decision
Exception	Approved deviation from policy or constraints

Future decision types SHALL extend this hierarchy.

7. Mandatory Decision Properties

Every Decision Record SHALL define:

Property	Description
Decision ID	Globally unique identifier
Decision Type	Canonical classification
Decision Statement	Formal engineering decision
Engineering Objective	Objective addressed
Decision Status	Proposed, Approved, Rejected, Superseded
Decision Authority	Human, Atlas, or Hybrid
Decision Timestamp	Time of decision
Effective Date	Date decision becomes effective
Expiration Date	Optional end of validity
8. Supporting Information

Every Decision Record SHALL reference:

Engineering Evidence
Engineering Understanding
Engineering Knowledge
Engineering Conclusions
Applicable Constraints
Applicable Policies
Confidence Assessment
Engineering Context

These references SHALL remain immutable.

9. Alternatives

Decision Records SHALL document evaluated alternatives where applicable.

Each alternative SHALL include:

Alternative identifier
Description
Advantages
Disadvantages
Evaluation summary
Reason for acceptance or rejection

Rejected alternatives SHALL remain part of the decision history.

10. Approval Model

Every Decision Record SHALL define its approval status.

Supported approval states include:

Draft
   │
Review
   │
Approved
   │
Implemented
   │
Superseded
   │
Archived

Approval authority SHALL be recorded.

11. Decision Provenance

Every Decision Record SHALL preserve:

Requesting workflow
Producing reasoning process
Supporting engineering artifacts
Decision authority
Human participants
Automated participants
Decision history

Decision provenance SHALL be immutable.

12. Decision Explainability

Every Decision Record SHALL include:

Engineering rationale
Supporting evidence
Assumptions
Constraints
Confidence
Alternative analysis
Policy references

Explainability SHALL be complete enough to reconstruct why the decision was made.

13. Decision Quality

Every Decision Record SHALL be evaluated for:

Correctness
Completeness
Consistency
Explainability
Traceability
Confidence
Policy compliance
Engineering validity

Quality assessments SHALL be versioned.

14. Revision Model

Engineering decisions MAY be revised.

A revised Decision Record SHALL:

Reference the superseded decision.
Preserve the original record.
Record revision reason.
Record revision authority.
Maintain complete traceability.

No Decision Record SHALL be modified in place.

15. Conformance

A Decision Record conforms if it:

Inherits the CAO contract.
Implements mandatory decision properties.
Preserves decision provenance.
Records approvals.
Documents alternatives.
Supports revision history.
16. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Collaborative decision records.
Multi-organization approvals.
Regulatory review workflows.
AI-assisted decision explanations.
Digital signature integration.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical decision semantics.

